1|0@Windows10^1439109074#ctwpzca:NotACommunist$:Personally I would describe my experience like this: When you switch to a new OS it's a little unfamiliar at first, you're used to the way things used to be. Then you use the new OS for a while and start to become familiar with it, you start to see why it's better than the previous stuff. Then you get to the point where you really can't imagine going back to the old OS, and that's where I'm at now. I came from Windows 7, and even though it was great I really wouldn't want to go back to it now. I just haven't run into any issues with it at all, not a single crash or blue screen in nearly 2 weeks of use. It's faster too, games run better, my PC boots SO MUCH FASTER it's not even funny, I like the improved UI for File Explorer and other things, it's really just all around better than 7 in my opinion.
1|0@Windows10^1439109539#ctwq3gl:tumas04$:The main problem with asking such a question: Most of the threads here are from people who have any kinds of problems with windows 10. Those people without any issues (which is probably the majority) either do not care about forums/reddit or do not post something like "Everything is great....". Thus most threads here are about problems and what did go wrong with the upgrade ;). Anyway for me everything worked great. Ok Nvidia has/had some troubles with its drivers and some tweaks to make windows 10 "better" were necessary on my side but else i am quite happy with windows 10. I like the look and i like the speed (especially on my old laptop; it runs better than windows 7/8). The force of updates and not beeing able to disable driver updates all together is probably the most annoying thing. But there is a tool for this kind of issue, so it's fine i guess.
